00:00:03.189 --> 00:00:06.389 align:start position:0%
bogota is a big  dirty and noisy city air pollution kills  about 2 000 people here every year  the air quality in the colombian capital  is often so bad  that an environmental state of emergency  has to be declared  greenhouse gas emissions linked to the  transporting of goods by road  contributes significantly to the problem  but that is sent to change in part  thanks to people such as daniel sanchez  he used to be part of the problem now  he's part of the solution  a pioneer of change on the streets of  bogota  i used to drive a truck there were  always two of us  everything took a long time because the  traffic jams and having to look for a  place to park  it was really tiring  now he drives an electric delivery  tricycle  this is the cross docking station at the  edge of the city center  goods are transferred here from large  trucks to the small eco-friendly  vehicles for delivery into downtown  the project was launched by the  municipality since last year the green  alliance has headed the city government  for the first time  transport is responsible for about 79 to  particulate emissions here  and 39 of that amount results from  transporting goods by road  by switching to these new vehicles we  can cut emissions by 16 metric tons a  day  so the impact will be huge  the world bank has made 300 000  available to the project  it's still early days during the current  pilot phase  the fleet of small electric delivery  vehicles numbers just  15.  they're the work of a startup in  medellin  but the model designed for that city  needs to be adapted to conditions in  bogota  the logistics are very different from  medellin  retailers aren't concentrated in one  district  distances are much larger so the wear  and tear on the electric components is  greater  we need to innovate and start working on  improving the motor and the battery  so we can provide the companies in the  project with an efficient solution  the lolla cargo tricycles can carry one  cubic meter of goods  that's about 1 8 the volume of a  standard delivery truck  daniel sanchez's destinations today are  small shops and pharmacies  he works on commission so for him time  is money  and he zips from one customer to the  next  he maneuvers his electric bike through  traffic jams  he's glad to be rid of his truck  traffic it's just the best thing  i can go bike lanes and sidewalks  anywhere  and i can take shortcuts  i can get to any point really fast  it's much better than before  not being stuck in traffic not having to  look for a parking place  makes delivering goods more efficient  several companies have signed up to the  project they not only want to cut their  harmful emissions but also save time and  money doing so  nutresa is a major manufacturer of  processed and convenient  foods it's an ideal candidate for taking  part in the pilot phase of the project  in colombia most of its products are  sold in small corner shops  and by street vendors  the pilot phase has been running for  just one month  but the results are already very  significant and very positive  six other companies in the city have  also joined the project  and i hope many more will too  using sustainable methods of transport  has many advantages  for people's quality of life the  environment  and the development of urban logistics  it's too soon to evaluate the pilot  project as a whole  but daniel sanchez for one hopes it will  prove a success  and set a precedent he says his work  days are a third  shorter now and his earnings higher he  also enjoys the attention  passers-by are fascinated  about people ask me is that your bike  and i say  sadly no it's not  they find it really cool and think it's  a good idea no exhaust  nothing that harms the environment i  feel i'm promoting change  he still takes the bus home but he's  saving up to buy a good bike  and then he will contribute even more to  the greening of bogota  you
 

